    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          More sealer is not more protection. Past a certain film thickness, additional coats stop adding durability and start actively causing the cracking and peeling they were meant to prevent — which is why the answer is almost always two thin coats, and almost never three.
        </p>

        <div id="how-many" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Coats You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two thin coats is the correct answer for the overwhelming majority of residential asphalt driveways being resealed on a normal maintenance cycle. The second coat is not there to add bulk — it is there to cover the pinholes, thin spots and skipped patches that are unavoidable in the first coat, and to build the film to a thickness that survives a freeze-thaw winter.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Routine reseal, surface in good shape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard maintenance film thickness</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">First seal on cured new asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tight surface, second coat evens the sheen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavily oxidized, very porous surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">First coat is absorbed, second builds the film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed within the last 12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Existing film is still sound, avoid buildup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Touch-up on a worn traffic lane only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot coat, feathered into the surrounding area</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Any driveway, ever</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never three</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buildup cracks; thickness stops helping</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The one situation people most often get wrong is the very porous driveway. Faced with asphalt that drinks the first coat and still looks grey in patches, the instinct is to add a third coat. What that surface actually needed was two coats applied at a normal rate, with the acceptance that a heavily weathered driveway will never come back to the deep uniform black of new pavement. Chasing that colour with extra coats is how buildup problems start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Two Thin Coats Beat One Thick Coat
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most counter-intuitive thing about driveway sealer, and it is worth understanding rather than just accepting, because understanding it changes how you apply the product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Curing Happens From the Top Down</h3>

          <p>
            Asphalt emulsion sealer cures by releasing water and solvent to the air. That release happens at the surface. A thin coat lets the entire film off-gas and set within a normal drying window. A thick coat skins over on top while the material underneath is still wet, and that skin then traps the remaining moisture and solvent below it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A coat that has skinned over a wet layer stays soft for days or weeks. It tracks onto tyres and shoe soles, it prints the tread pattern of every car that parks on it, and when it finally does cure it does so unevenly, leaving internal stresses that show up as cracking the following winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Coats Cover Different Defects</h3>

          <p>
            No single application pass is perfect. There are always pinholes where air escaped, thin spots where the applicator lifted slightly, and skipped patches along edges and around obstacles. The value of the second coat is that its defects fall in different places than the first coat’s defects. Applied perpendicular to the first, it covers what the first missed, and the combined film has no path straight through to the asphalt.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Perpendicular Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Run the second coat across the first, not along it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the first coat was pulled from the garage toward the street, pull the second from side to side. Squeegee and brush marks run in the direction of travel, and crossing them means the second coat’s marks cannot line up with the first coat’s. It is a small habit that makes a visible difference in the finished uniformity.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thin Coats Flex, Thick Coats Crack</h3>

          <p>
            Asphalt moves. It expands in summer heat and contracts in winter cold, and the sealer film on top has to move with it. A thin, well-cured film has enough flexibility to follow that movement. A thick, incompletely cured slab of sealer is comparatively brittle, and when the asphalt underneath moves, the film cracks — usually in a fine web pattern that then admits the water it was supposed to keep out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-not-three" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why a Third Coat Makes Things Worse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buildup is a real and common failure mode, and it is entirely self-inflicted. It comes from applying too much sealer at once, or from resealing far too often, and the result looks like a driveway that is peeling in sheets or cracking in a fine alligator pattern that has nothing to do with the asphalt beneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mechanism is straightforward. Each coat of sealer is a layer of relatively rigid material bonded to a flexible substrate. Add enough layers and the combined film becomes thick enough that it can no longer flex with the asphalt. The stresses have to go somewhere, and they go into cracking the sealer film itself. Once that film cracks, water gets underneath it, and freeze-thaw cycling then lifts it off the driveway in plates.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why over-sealing on an annual schedule is worse than sealing on a two to three year cycle. Each fresh coat goes onto a film that has not yet worn thin, and the total thickness climbs every year until it fails. A driveway that has been sealed every spring for six years will often be in visibly worse condition than one sealed twice in the same period.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">How to Recognise Sealer Buildup</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Peeling in sheets, not wearing in patches.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Normal wear thins the sealer gradually and reveals grey asphalt underneath, most obviously in the tyre tracks. Buildup failure looks different: black flakes and plates lifting away from the surface, often with more black sealer visible underneath them. If you can pick up a piece of your driveway’s finish and it is thick enough to hold its shape, you have buildup.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Two Coats Actually Means in Gallons
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealcoat is designed to go on thin. The working figure is roughly one gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat, which puts a standard 4.75-gallon pail at around 400 square feet for a single coat. That number moves with surface condition — a heavily oxidized, porous driveway will pull toward the 80 square foot end, a smooth one toward 100.
          </p>

          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest mt-8 mb-3">Two-coat material estimates</p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Two Coats, Smooth</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Two Coats, Porous</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single car, short</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two car, standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two car, long</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">900 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Three car or extended</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-8 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy for the porous column if you have any doubt. Running out of sealer partway through a second coat is a genuinely bad outcome — the boundary between the two-coat area and the one-coat area will be visible for the life of the job, and a mid-project trip to the store can easily push you past the weather window. Unopened pails can usually be returned.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a more precise figure based on your actual measurements rather than these bands, work through the numbers on our driveway sealer calculator, which accounts for surface porosity and adds a sensible waste allowance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long to Wait Between Coats
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first coat must be dry before the second goes on. Not cured — dry. Those are different states, and confusing them causes a lot of failed sealing jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dry to the touch typically takes four to eight hours in good conditions, meaning temperatures comfortably above 50°F, low humidity and some air movement. At that point the surface will not transfer to a finger pressed firmly against it, and the second coat can go on. Full cure, the point at which vehicles can return, takes 24 to 48 hours after the final coat.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply the first coat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Early in the day, so it has maximum daylight and warmth to dry in. Work from the garage outward toward the street.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Wait until dry to the touch</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Typically 4-8 hours. Press firmly with a finger in a shaded area, which dries slowest. Nothing should transfer.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply the second coat perpendicular</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Across the direction of the first coat, at the same thin rate. Do not compensate for a thin-looking first coat by going heavy here.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Keep everything off for the full cure</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Foot traffic once dry to the touch, vehicles after 24-48 hours per the product label. Longer in cool or humid weather.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            In practice this makes driveway sealing a two-day project on a settled weekend rather than a Saturday afternoon job. Plan for it. The most common reason people end up applying one thick coat instead of two thin ones is that they started too late in the day and did not want to leave the job half done.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Weather Governs Everything</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You need 24 to 48 dry hours after the final coat.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rain on an uncured coat washes it away, and that includes overnight dew in marginal conditions. Overnight lows need to stay above 50°F for the cure to complete properly. Check the extended forecast before you open the first pail, not after.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does the Sealer Type Change How Many Coats You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Broadly, no. Two thin coats is the right answer across asphalt emulsion, coal tar and acrylic products alike, because the reasoning behind it — covering the first coat’s pinholes, and keeping each layer thin enough to cure fully — is about physics rather than chemistry. What changes between products is how thick a single coat lands and how long you wait between them.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Note on Application</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard. Dries to touch in 4-8 hours in good conditions</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coal tar emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Similar timing; ventilate well and wear respiratory protection</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based / cutback</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Much slower cure; allow considerably longer between coats</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thinner bodied, so the second coat matters more, not less</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Filler-and-sealer combo</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mix thoroughly; filler solids settle heavily in the pail</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The one meaningful variation is cure time between coats. Oil-based cutback sealers work by penetrating and rejuvenating dried-out asphalt rather than forming a film on top, and they release solvent slowly. Rushing a second coat onto a cutback product traps that solvent and leaves a surface that stays soft for weeks. Read the label rather than assuming the four-to-eight-hour window that applies to emulsions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premium high-solids products are sometimes marketed in a way that implies a single coat is sufficient because each coat is thicker. Treat that claim carefully. A higher solids content does build more film per pass, but it does not eliminate the pinholes and applicator skips that the second coat exists to cover. Two coats of a premium product is a genuinely excellent job; one coat of it is still one coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Heavy Coat to Save a Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the mistake that produces every other sealing problem. A heavy coat skins over on top while staying wet underneath, stays tacky for days, prints tyre treads, and cures with internal stresses that crack the following winter. Two thin coats over two days is not a stylistic preference — it is how the product is designed to be used.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a Third Coat to Chase Colour</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavily weathered driveway will not return to the deep uniform black of fresh pavement, and no number of coats will make it. Extra coats past two add thickness without adding protection, and thickness is what causes buildup cracking and sheet peeling. Accept two coats and the colour they give you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing Every Single Year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Annual sealing does not keep a driveway in better condition. It stacks film on top of film that has not worn thin yet, and the accumulated thickness eventually cracks and lifts. The normal interval for asphalt emulsion is two to three years. Reseal when the surface has genuinely worn grey, not on a calendar reminder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying the Second Coat Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting a second coat over a first that is still wet creates exactly the same trapped-moisture problem as a single thick coat, with the added disadvantage that the surface looked finished when you did it. Wait until the first coat is properly dry to the touch, including in the shaded areas that dry slowest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Only Enough for One Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage figures printed on a pail generally describe a single coat on a favourable surface. Doubling for two coats and adding margin for porosity is the realistic calculation. Running out mid-job leaves a permanent visible boundary between the two-coat and one-coat areas.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of driveway sealer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats for almost every residential asphalt driveway on a normal maintenance cycle. The second coat covers the pinholes and thin spots of the first and builds the film to a thickness that survives freeze-thaw cycling. One coat is enough only for a touch-up or a driveway sealed within the last year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is two coats of driveway sealer better than one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, substantially. A single coat always contains pinholes, thin spots and skipped patches, and it wears through at the high points within a season or two. Two thin coats applied perpendicular to each other cover each other’s defects and produce a film with no direct path through to the asphalt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put three coats of sealer on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but you should not. Past two coats, additional sealer stops adding protection and starts causing buildup, where the accumulated film becomes too rigid to flex with the asphalt underneath and cracks or peels away in sheets. Two coats is the practical maximum for a single application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long between coats of driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wait until the first coat is dry to the touch, typically four to eight hours in warm, dry conditions with good air movement. Test in a shaded area, which dries slowest. After the final coat, allow 24 to 48 hours before driving on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much sealer do I need for two coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on roughly one gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat, so a 4.75-gallon pail covers about 400 square feet in one coat. A standard 600 square foot two-car driveway needs about three pails for two coats on a smooth surface, four on a porous one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you put too much sealer on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It skins over on the surface while staying wet underneath, remains tacky for days or weeks, prints tyre treads, and cures with internal stresses that crack in a fine web pattern during the first freeze-thaw cycle. Over time, repeated over-application leads to sealer lifting off the driveway in plates.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

