        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two thin coats. That is the answer for nearly every asphalt driveway, applied perpendicular to each other, with the first fully dry to the touch before the second goes down and 24 to 48 hours of dry weather above 50°F after the last one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Resist the two temptations that ruin sealing jobs. Do not compress two coats into one heavy application to finish in a day — that produces a soft, tacky, tyre-printed surface that cracks by spring. And do not add a third coat chasing a deeper black on weathered asphalt, because thickness past two coats stops protecting and starts peeling.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy enough material up front for two coats plus margin, roughly a gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat with the porous end of that range as your planning figure. Running out halfway through the second coat leaves a boundary you will see for years.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
